<DocsLayout title="Security model and RC1 limitations" description="Review RelayBridge management, private-LAN SMTP, device authorization, queue, Microsoft identity, outbound TLS, support, and servicing boundaries." section="Security">
  <section>
    <h2>Security comes from several narrow boundaries</h2>
    <p>RelayBridge does not rely on one control or claim that every risk is eliminated. RC1 combines a loopback management boundary, one explicit private SMTP listener, per-device authorization, durable local storage, certificate-based Microsoft identity, sender-scoped Exchange RBAC, and certificate-validated outbound TLS.</p>
    <div class="docs-table-wrap">
      <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Boundary</th><th>Implemented control</th><th>Administrator responsibility or residual risk</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Management</th><td>Fixed HTTP loopback binding; no remote management; opener accepts no arbitrary URL</td><td>Protect the Windows session and local Administrator access</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Device LAN</th><td>One explicit private listener; no wildcard or public binding; source and sender allowlists</td><td>Isolate the LAN, restrict firewall scope, and accept that inbound TLS is absent</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Authenticated devices</th><td>One-time generated secret, salted verifier, and bounded authentication attempts</td><td>Protect the displayed password and device configuration; credentials cross the private LAN in cleartext</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Legacy devices</th><td>No password, but public and unrestricted source rules are rejected</td><td>Reserve stable private addresses and prevent spoofing or untrusted LAN access</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Queue</th><td>Streamed spool, durable flush, atomic promotion, and SQLite commit before local <code>250</code></td><td>Protect ProgramData, monitor capacity, and plan backup and retention</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Microsoft identity</th><td>Customer-owned single-tenant app and non-exportable local certificate; no client secret</td><td>Monitor certificate lifecycle and tenant-side changes</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Exchange authorization</th><td>Sender group, management scope, and scoped <code>Application SMTP.SendAsApp</code></td><td>Keep the selected mailbox and RBAC objects under change control</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Outbound SMTP</th><td>Fixed Exchange endpoint, mandatory STARTTLS, platform certificate validation, and XOAUTH2</td><td>Permit the required network path without bypassing TLS checks</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Privileged operations</th><td>Short-lived fixed helpers, bounded one-shot state, and narrow allowlisted operations</td><td>Review each elevation prompt and recognize that a local Administrator can change installed state</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Diagnostics/export</th><td>Bounded status schemas and a local allowlisted support bundle</td><td>Review operational metadata before sharing</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Servicing</th><td>Per-machine WiX/MSI service, ACL, repair, upgrade, and uninstall behavior</td><td>Deliberately retain or remove ProgramData, certificates, and tenant objects</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
